#
# Trivy misconfiguration suppressions.
# Reference: https://trivy.dev/latest/docs/configuration/filtering/
#
# The shared scan workflow discovers this file at the repository root and passes
# it to trivy as --ignorefile. A config-file `ignorefile:` key in
# .github/scan_tools_configs/trivy.yml would NOT work: trivy resolves that
# against its own working directory, which is the directory above the scanned
# checkout.
#
# This is trivy's plain-text format: a bare check ID disables that check
# EVERYWHERE, with no path scoping or expiry. So it is reserved for a check that
# no file in this repository could reasonably satisfy. Everything that is wrong
# for one file but worth catching in another is handled per file by
# scan.skip-files in .github/scan_tools_configs/trivy.yml instead.
#
# (Path-scoped, expiring suppressions would need trivy's `.trivyignore.yaml`
# format, which the shared workflow does not yet discover -- it only looks for
# the plain-text `.trivyignore`. Tracked upstream in rocm-security-gh; when it
# lands, the entry below should become a path-scoped KSV-0014 with an expiry.)

# Root filesystem is not read-only (readOnlyRootFilesystem). On a clean checkout
# KSV-0014 fires on 24 files; all but three are demonstration manifests,
# kustomize patch fragments, e2e fixtures, or the single vendored test pod, and
# are handled per file by scan.skip-files. The three that remain are the
# operator's own Go control-plane Deployments:
#     config/manager-base/manager.yaml
#     hack/kmmConfig/manager-base/manager.yaml
#     hack/kmmConfig/webhook-server/deployment.yaml
# These are controller-runtime managers; the proper fix is to set
# readOnlyRootFilesystem: true on them and drop this entry, pending validation
# on a cluster that none of them write to the container root. Until then this
# keeps the gate green without masking KSV-0014 on any other file.
AVD-KSV-0014
